# Search, feeds and llms.txt

A search index, an RSS feed and an `llms.txt` file all want the words of a page without its markup. Import a document with the `?text` query and you get the text a reader sees, split at each heading, with nothing compiled to Svelte:

```js
import text from './guide.svx?text';

text.title; // 'Getting started', the first # heading
text.text; // every block of the page, a blank line between two
text.sections; // [{ id: 'install', heading: 'Install', depth: 2, text: 'Run pnpm add mdsvex@next.' }, ...]
```

Each section starts at a heading and runs to the next one. With [`heading_ids`](/docs/reference/options#heading_ids) on, it carries the heading's `id`, so a search hit links to `page#id`. Text before the first heading is a section of its own, with an empty `heading` and `depth` 0, and only when there is some. `title` is the text of the first `#` heading outside any container, and is left out when there is none. It does not read the frontmatter, which is what [`?metadata`](/docs/guides/frontmatter) is for. The whole value is plain JSON.

A `?text` import parses the document and runs its plugins, and waits for the work they [defer](/docs/plugins/writing/deferred-work), as `?data` does, so the text has what plugins wrote: emoji, the text of links `references` made, citation labels. In dev an edit reloads what imports it only when the text changed, so an edit of the frontmatter alone leaves it be. For TypeScript, `mdsvex/globals` types it as `DocumentText`.

::marks

## Query imports

| import | gives | reads | an edit reloads it when |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `./post.svx` | the component, `metadata`, `plugin_data` | everything, and compiles it | anything changed |
| [`?metadata`](/docs/guides/frontmatter) | `metadata` | the frontmatter | the frontmatter changed |
| [`?data`](/docs/plugins/writing/plugin-data) | `metadata` and `plugin_data` | the document, with plugins, no render | either changed |
| `?text` | the text, by section | the document, with plugins, no render | the text changed |
| `?raw`, from Vite | the source as a string | the file | anything changed |

Each works with `import.meta.glob`: `import.meta.glob('./*.svx', { query: '?text', import: 'default', eager: true })`.

## What counts as text

| written | in the text |
| --- | --- |
| paragraphs, headings, list items, block quotes | their text, each a block |
| table cells | a row is a block, its cells on one line |
| emphasis, links, `~~strikethrough~~`, `^sup^`, `~sub~` | their text, a link without its url |
| images | the alt text, where the image is |
| code spans and inline math | as written, they are part of the sentence |
| code fences, `math` fences, `<pre>` | as written, each a block, lines kept. `text: { code_blocks: false }` leaves them out |
| footnotes | each referenced footnote at the end, in the order the page numbers them, the reference marks left out |
| directives | the label, and what a container holds. A plugin can say what its directive reads as, `:cite[key]` reads `[1]` |
| HTML elements and components | their text. A block element such as `<div>` or `<p>`, and a component among blocks, is a block |
| `{#if}`, `{#each}` and other blocks | the text of every branch, as which one shows is only known when the page runs |
| `{expression}`, `{@html}`, `{@render}` | nothing, they are only known when the page runs |
| frontmatter, `<script>`, `<style>`, `<svelte:head>`, comments | nothing |

Runs of spaces and line ends inside a block are one space, a hard break (a backslash at the end of a line) is a line end, blocks are apart by a blank line, and no line ends in a space. Character references such as `&amp;`, `&#233;` and the common named ones are their characters. Punctuation is as typed: [`smart_punctuation`](/docs/reference/options#smart_punctuation) changes the page only, so the text has the straight `'` a search box gets where the page shows a curly one.

## Recipes

::::plate[A search index](mark="find", tone=expr)
Build the records once, at build time, and load them into [FlexSearch][flexsearch] in the page that searches. Each section is a record, so with [`heading_ids`](/docs/reference/options#heading_ids) on a hit goes to its heading. Without it a section has no `id`, and every hit on a page links to the top of the page:

```js
// vite.config.js
mdsvex({ heading_ids: true });
```

```js
// src/routes/search.json/+server.js
import { json } from '@sveltejs/kit';

const pages = import.meta.glob('../docs/**/+page.svx', {
	query: '?text',
	import: 'default',
	eager: true
});

export const prerender = true;

export function GET() {
	const records = [];
	for (const [path, { title, sections }] of Object.entries(pages)) {
		const route = path.slice('..'.length, -'/+page.svx'.length);
		for (const section of sections) {
			records.push({
				id: records.length,
				href: section.id ? `${route}#${section.id}` : route,
				title,
				heading: section.heading,
				text: section.text
			});
		}
	}
	return json(records);
}
```

```js
// in the search component
import { Document } from 'flexsearch';

const index = new Document({
	document: { id: 'id', index: ['heading', 'text'], store: ['href', 'title', 'heading'] }
});
const records = await (await fetch('/search.json')).json();
for (const record of records) index.add(record);

const hits = index.search(query, { enrich: true, merge: true });
```

Each record gets an `id` of its own rather than using its `href`. Two sections can share an `href`, as every section of a page does without `heading_ids`, and FlexSearch keeps one record for each id.

[Pagefind][pagefind] indexes the built HTML instead, and needs none of this.
::::

::::plate[An RSS feed](mark="rss", tone=link)
`?metadata` gives the title and date, `?text` the body. A feed needs absolute links, and a prerendered route has no request to take the origin from, so `url.origin` is `http://sveltekit-prerender` unless the config names the site's origin:

```js
// vite.config.js
sveltekit({ paths: { origin: 'https://example.com' } });
```

```js
// src/routes/rss.xml/+server.js
import { resolve } from '$app/paths';

const metadata = import.meta.glob('../blog/*/+page.svx', {
	query: '?metadata',
	import: 'metadata',
	eager: true
});
const texts = import.meta.glob('../blog/*/+page.svx', {
	query: '?text',
	import: 'default',
	eager: true
});

export const prerender = true;

const escape = (text) =>
	text.replace(/[&<>]/g, (c) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;' })[c]);

export function GET({ url }) {
	const items = Object.entries(metadata)
		// a post without a title and a date in its frontmatter is left out
		.filter(([, post]) => typeof post?.title === 'string' && post.date)
		.map(([path, { title, date }]) => {
			const link = new URL(resolve(`/blog/${path.split('/')[2]}`), url).href;
			return `<item>
	<title>${escape(title)}</title>
	<link>${link}</link>
	<guid>${link}</guid>
	<pubDate>${new Date(date).toUTCString()}</pubDate>
	<description>${escape(texts[path].text)}</description>
</item>`;
		});
	return new Response(
		`<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0"><channel>
<title>My blog</title>
<link>${new URL(resolve('/'), url).href}</link>
<description>Posts</description>
${items.join('\n')}
</channel></rss>`,
		{ headers: { 'content-type': 'application/rss+xml' } }
	);
}
```

:::notes
Each post needs a `title` and a `date` in its frontmatter, the date in a form `new Date` reads, such as `2026-03-14`. A document with no frontmatter has `undefined` metadata, which the filter leaves out instead of failing the build. `resolve` from `$app/paths` puts `paths.base` in front of a route, and `new URL` makes it absolute against the url of the feed.
:::

An Atom feed is the same with `<entry>` and `<summary>`. For a feed with the HTML of each post, render the component with `render` from `svelte/server` instead.
::::

::::plate[llms.txt](mark="llms", tone=text)
[`llms.txt`][llms-txt] is markdown: a title, a summary and a list of links. `?text` gives each link its title and a first paragraph. The links are absolute, so set `paths.origin` as for the feed:

```js
// src/routes/llms.txt/+server.js
import { resolve } from '$app/paths';

const pages = import.meta.glob('../docs/**/+page.svx', {
	query: '?text',
	import: 'default',
	eager: true
});

export const prerender = true;

export function GET({ url }) {
	const links = Object.entries(pages).map(([path, { title, sections }]) => {
		const route = path.slice('..'.length, -'/+page.svx'.length);
		const href = new URL(resolve(route), url).href;
		const summary = sections[0]?.text.split('\n\n')[0];
		return `- [${title ?? route}](${href})${summary ? ': ' + summary : ''}`;
	});
	return new Response(
		`# My site\n\n> What the site is for, in a sentence.\n\n## Docs\n\n${links.join('\n')}\n`,
		{ headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8' } }
	);
}
```

For `llms-full.txt`, which holds whole pages, the markdown itself usually reads better than plain text, as it keeps the headings, lists and code. Vite's `?raw` already gives the source of a document, frontmatter and Svelte included. Use `?text` there when you want only what a reader sees.
::::

## Text with `compile`

::::plate[`compile_text`](mark="text", tone=expr)
`compile_text` from `mdsvex/compile` gives the same value without Vite. It takes the options `compile` takes, and `code_blocks`, and waits for deferred work as [`compile_async`](/docs/reference/compile#compile_async) does:

```js
import { compile_text } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const { title, text, sections } = await compile_text(source, {
	heading_ids: true,
	plugins: [citations()],
	code_blocks: false
});
```

Compiling [in steps](/docs/guides/without-vite#compiling-in-steps), `parsed_text(parsed, { plugins })` reads a `Parsed` once it settled, and the document can still render afterwards. `text_module` from `mdsvex/host` makes the module a `?text` import loads from that value.
::::

::::plate[What a directive reads as](mark=":::", tone=directive)
A plugin says what one of its directives reads as with `text` beside its handlers. It gets the directive's args once the plugins settled, and the text of its label, and returns a string, or `undefined` to read the label and what the directive holds. A leaf or a container reads the string as blocks, apart where it has a blank line:

```js
{
	name: 'cite',
	module: '@acme/cite/directives',
	directives: {
		cite: {
			text: (args, label) => (args.number ? `[${args.number}]` : label),
			parse(node, ctx) { /* sets args.number */ }
		}
	}
}
```
::::
